    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.33774/miir-2026-h9224",
    "abstract": "This report addresses a challenge, proposed at the 181th European Study Group in Industry, that aimed to develop models that could anticipate and adapt to the impacts of climate change on the Vinho Verde region, concerning the production of wine. In this sequel and leveraging historical meteorological data and grapevine growth stages, regression models, able to infer consequences of future weather changes, were constructed. The analysis of acidity levels, alcohol content, and chemical analyses of wine samples, allowed to assess how climatic vari- ations affect wine composition. This study intends to synthesize this information in order to create future scenarios for the wine industry. By identifying trends and understanding the interplay of different factors, it is possible to assist vineyard owners in making informed decisions and adapting to evolving climates.",
    "field": "Data-driven modeling of climate change impacts on viticulture",
    "problem": "This paper addresses the challenge of predicting climate change impacts on Vinho Verde vineyards by developing data-driven models that integrate localized climate data with grape physicochemical measurements, a gap left by previous studies that typically examined these domains separately.",
    "method": "This study proposes data-driven regression models that integrate historical meteorological records with grape physicochemical data to predict harvest timing and wine composition, thereby anticipating the impacts of climate change on Vinho Verde vineyards.",
    "task": "The task is to develop data-driven models using historical meteorological and grape physicochemical data to forecast the impacts of climate change on grape characteristics and harvest timing in Portugal's Vinho Verde wine region.",
